Title:
JonMon-Lite (Remote Agentless EDR via ETW Data Collector Sets)

Type:
GitHub Tool

Short Summary (4-8 sentences max):
JonMon-Lite is a proof-of-concept “remote agentless EDR” that remotely creates and starts an ETW trace session using Windows Data Collector Sets (DCS), producing ETL logs without deploying an endpoint agent. It solves the problem of collecting high-fidelity telemetry from Windows hosts when you have admin credentials but don’t want to install software or drivers. The tool uses an XML-defined collector set, a JSON config for targets/paths/credentials, and installs ETW manifest files to enable event parsing/visibility. It collects security-relevant telemetry such as process creation, file creation, .NET runtime loads, WMI activity, RPC client/server calls, DPAPI CryptUnprotectData, and AMSI events. This is useful for red teams and pentesters for stealthy/low-footprint monitoring and for blue teams/researchers to understand how ETW/DCS can be abused for remote telemetry collection. It’s interesting because it demonstrates a built-in Windows mechanism that can approximate EDR-like visibility over the network using administrative access and standard logging infrastructure.

Technical Focus:
- ETW trace sessions and ETL collection
- Data Collector Sets (Performance Logs) creation/management (local/remote)
- Remote administrative access via UNC paths (e.g., C$) and credentialed operations
- ETW manifests/providers and event consumption
- Telemetry sources: Kernel-Process/Kernel-File, .NET Runtime, WMI-Activity, RPC, DPAPI, AMSI

Use Cases:
- Remote host telemetry collection without deploying an agent
- Post-compromise monitoring of process/file activity across multiple workstations
- Researching “living-off-the-land” logging/telemetry mechanisms and their abuse paths
- Building detections around suspicious remote DCS/ETW collector creation and ETL staging
- Incident response triage by pulling ETL traces from endpoints centrally

Keywords:
ETW, Event Tracing for Windows, Data Collector Set, DCS, ETL, PerfLogs, Microsoft-Windows-Kernel-Process, Microsoft-Windows-Kernel-File, Microsoft-Windows-DotNETRuntime, Microsoft-Windows-WMI-Activity, Microsoft-Windows-RPC, Microsoft-Windows-Crypto-DPAPI, CryptUnprotectData, AMSI, Microsoft-Antimalware-Scan-Interface, remote telemetry, UNC path, C$ admin share, agentless EDR